From: Caboose <caboose@local>
Subject: [PATCH 94/99] Start a process without asking what its session already
 knows

A GL start through Zink waited for the phone about 36 times, each a round trip at the
phone's latency; Steam starts about ten such processes one after another. Most of those
answers were known before the process asked.

Instance extensions and the physical device count are kept in the executor session's
instance scope, so each process neither lists them nor counts again. A session has one
GPU: once a process has asked its identity, later processes whose every instance finds one
physical device take the session's identity for theirs at enumeration, so the capability
answers kept for it (properties, device extensions) are found before anything is asked.
The host's features answer for the chain sent (the structures the driver answers itself
taken out), its memory properties and its sparse image format answers are kept in that
scope too; the driver's own adjustments apply to a kept answer as to an asked one. A
question looked up for a process that may never answer it is peeked, never claimed, so
no other process waits for it.

What the device can write is now followed (caboose-bounded-memory/device_work.h): every
queue command, staged submissions included, and every host-side copy or layout transition
counts. An invalidate of memory nothing was sent since its allocation could have written
reads nothing: its mapped bytes are what the process put there, and new memory's contents
are undefined. Memory allocated with contents of its own (zero initialization, a capture
replay address) is always read, and memory with GPU writes marked or blocks left lazy is
never taken for unwritten. A vkQueueWaitIdle on a queue nothing was sent to since the
device last reported it idle, or ever, returns at once. Zink invalidated six ranges and
waited for an idle queue three times at every start, before it had submitted anything.

CABOOSE_READ_PROFILE=all reports every command that blocked, not only the eight slowest.
